}
</style>
<small>shape: (4, 5)</small><table border="1" class="dataframe"><thead><tr><th>study</th><th>measurable</th><th>rank</th><th>share</th><th>label</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>list[bool]</td><td>list[u32]</td><td>list[f64]</td><td>list[str]</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;CT-1&quot;</td><td>[true, false, true]</td><td>[2, 3, 1]</td><td>[0.33, 0.25, 0.42]</td><td>[&quot;target&quot;, &quot;small&quot;, &quot;target&quot;]</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;CT-2&quot;</td><td>[false]</td><td>[1]</td><td>[1.0]</td><td>[&quot;small&quot;]</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;CT-3&quot;</td><td>[]</td><td>[]</td><td>[]</td><td>[]</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;CT-4&quot;</td><td>null</td><td>null</td><td>null</td><td>null</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
```
:::
:::


An aggregation inside `eval()` still returns a list (of one). `list.agg()`
unwraps it: a scalar result becomes a plain column, anything else stays a
list. As in `group_by().agg()` (@sec-grp-list-trap), the decision is static:
it depends on the expression, not on the data.

::: {#f64486ee .cell execution_count=8}
``` {.python .cell-code}
lesions_df.select(
    "study",
    eval_sum=pl.col("long_mm").list.eval(pl.element().sum()),
    agg_sum=pl.col("long_mm").list.agg(pl.element().sum()),
    agg_n_big=pl.col("long_mm").list.agg((pl.element() >= 10).sum()),
    agg_big=pl.col("long_mm").list.agg(pl.element().filter(pl.element() >= 10)),
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=8}
```{=html}
